        <body>
            <div xml:id="d96">

                <pb facs="0245" n="145" xml:id="pg145"/>

                <head>No. 96.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Frelinghuysen to Mr. Morton.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Department of State</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1884-03-18"><hi rendition="#italic">March</hi> 18,
                        1884</date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 474]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: With reference to your dispatch No. 494, of
                    the 5th ultimo, in which you make a full statement of the facts relating to the
                    case of Alfred P. Jacob, I have to say that the previous instruction was based
                    on the absence of any report from the legation in the premises and the
                    assumption that Mr. Jacob had voluntarily submitted to serve in the army without
                    recourse to diplomatic protection. His case now, as it stands, is peculiar. By
                    our laws he is an American citizen, his status as such dating from his father’s
                    naturalization.</p>
                <p>By French law he is a recognized citizen through service in the army, which only
                    a citizen can perform. That obligation to do military duty (whether rightly due
                    or not) having been extinguished, no precise issue remains on which the status
                    of Mr. Jacob could be carried before a court. To make the issue, the only
                    available way would seem to be to present the case and ask that his name be
                    stricken off. With this explanation you may make the request contemplated in our
                    No. 436.</p>
                <p>I am, &amp;c.,</p>
                <closer><signed>FRED’K T. FRELINGHUYSEN.</signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
